WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.086 --> 00:00:02.236
- Hey everybody, this is Gary Vaynerchuk,

00:00:02.236 --> 00:00:05.696
and this is episode 320
of The #AskGaryVee show,

00:00:05.696 --> 00:00:07.976
and this is a very exciting episode,

00:00:07.976 --> 00:00:10.916
because the person that will
be dialing the questions

00:00:10.916 --> 00:00:13.406
and getting to the
questions today is India,

00:00:13.406 --> 00:00:16.686
our very own India, who's on her last day

00:00:16.686 --> 00:00:20.666
after seven years at Vayner
Media, moving to Portland.

00:00:20.666 --> 00:00:23.616
So everybody in Portland,
if you see her, stop her,

00:00:23.616 --> 00:00:25.666
take a selfie, post it on social, tag me.

00:00:26.766 --> 00:00:29.696
But fun fact, for a lot
of you that don't know,

00:00:29.696 --> 00:00:32.349
India was a superstar.

00:00:34.208 --> 00:00:37.236
You know how Ari Gold actually
became the star of Entourage,

00:00:37.236 --> 00:00:38.766
but that wasn't the purpose?

00:00:38.766 --> 00:00:42.407
There was absolutely a time in 2016?

00:00:42.407 --> 00:00:43.240
- '16 I think?

00:00:43.240 --> 00:00:47.386
- In 2016, where India was
my main cohost of the show,

00:00:47.386 --> 00:00:49.176
when it was a completely call-in show,

00:00:49.176 --> 00:00:51.706
or take questions from the internet show,

00:00:51.706 --> 00:00:54.446
and the reason I had
to get her off the show

00:00:54.446 --> 00:00:57.596
was it became clear to me that
she'd become bigger than me,

00:00:57.596 --> 00:00:59.896
and I wasn't really
willing to deal with that,

00:01:01.226 --> 00:01:03.706
and has just been an
amazing part of our team,

00:01:03.706 --> 00:01:06.009
and I love her very much, and
it's fun to have you here.

00:01:07.147 --> 00:01:08.536
Do you know the first episode you were on?

00:01:08.536 --> 00:01:10.452
- I think it was like
number 20 or something.

00:01:10.452 --> 00:01:11.285
- 20?
- Yeah.

00:01:11.285 --> 00:01:12.118
- That's amazing.

00:01:12.118 --> 00:01:13.136
So, it's fun to have you here.

00:01:13.136 --> 00:01:15.816
For all the new cats, go back
and watch some of those shows,

00:01:15.816 --> 00:01:19.216
like episode 30 to 200, or 150,

00:01:19.216 --> 00:01:21.996
and you'll see a bunch of
India and I interactions.

00:01:21.996 --> 00:01:23.446
It's a lot of fun, so thanks for being on.

00:01:23.446 --> 00:01:26.436
Also exciting, we have
the marketing fire power

00:01:26.436 --> 00:01:27.739
behind NASCAR,

00:01:28.896 --> 00:01:31.126
an organization that we've,
at the VaynerX level,

00:01:31.126 --> 00:01:32.486
have worked with a little bit,

00:01:32.486 --> 00:01:36.946
but more importantly,
just my love of sports

00:01:36.946 --> 00:01:39.426
and marketing, I'm
always looking for guests

00:01:39.426 --> 00:01:41.146
that make that cross,

00:01:41.146 --> 00:01:43.106
so it's super exciting
to have you guys here.

00:01:43.106 --> 00:01:44.266
Why don't you guys introduce yourselves

00:01:44.266 --> 00:01:46.276
to the Vayner Nation,
and before you do that,

00:01:46.276 --> 00:01:48.366
put in your phone numbers
on YouTube and Facebook

00:01:48.366 --> 00:01:49.736
if you wanna call in and ask questions,

00:01:49.736 --> 00:01:51.886
I'm gonna go pretty question-heavy here,

00:01:51.886 --> 00:01:53.586
especially if you're
into sports marketing,

00:01:53.586 --> 00:01:56.996
into NASCAR itself, or marketing itself,

00:01:56.996 --> 00:01:59.156
as you start listening to the framework.

00:01:59.156 --> 00:02:00.206
Ladies first.

00:02:00.206 --> 00:02:01.039
- Thank you.

00:02:01.039 --> 00:02:03.116
I'm Jill Gregory, I'm the
chief marketing officer

00:02:03.116 --> 00:02:04.906
at NASCAR, I've been at NASCAR,

00:02:04.906 --> 00:02:09.359
Steve Phelps here hired
me 10-plus years ago.

00:02:10.396 --> 00:02:13.306
Big fan of the sport,
but was not growing up,

00:02:13.306 --> 00:02:15.466
and I think the potential
that we've got at NASCAR

00:02:15.466 --> 00:02:18.026
to keep growing is huge,
so thanks for having us.

00:02:18.026 --> 00:02:18.859
- [Gary] Of course, Jill.

00:02:18.859 --> 00:02:21.726
- And I'm Steve Phelps, I'm
the president of NASCAR,

00:02:21.726 --> 00:02:25.296
and I've been with NASCAR almost 14 years,

00:02:25.296 --> 00:02:28.236
I think 14 years next week.

00:02:28.236 --> 00:02:30.486
I did grow up a NASCAR fan,

00:02:30.486 --> 00:02:32.476
worked at the NFL for almost 14 years,

00:02:32.476 --> 00:02:34.116
and then transitioned over to NASCAR,

00:02:34.116 --> 00:02:37.696
and it's a phenomenal place
to work and a great sport.

00:02:37.696 --> 00:02:38.586
- Let's lean into this,

00:02:38.586 --> 00:02:39.966
because I think, for
everybody who's watching,

00:02:39.966 --> 00:02:41.496
to make a connection point
with the Vayner Nation

00:02:41.496 --> 00:02:43.646
and you guys, I'm obsessed.

00:02:43.646 --> 00:02:46.736
My whole last decade of my
career is to build a machine,

00:02:46.736 --> 00:02:48.766
this marketing communications machine,

00:02:48.766 --> 00:02:51.099
to buy nostalgic businesses and brands.

00:02:52.036 --> 00:02:56.036
And what's been really fun,
being 43 and loving sports,

00:02:56.036 --> 00:03:00.516
is there's been incredible
patterns in NASCAR,

00:03:00.516 --> 00:03:04.176
in culture, as a net sport,
over the last 50 years,

00:03:04.176 --> 00:03:05.786
for that matter.

00:03:05.786 --> 00:03:07.456
I wanna go into that origin story.

00:03:07.456 --> 00:03:10.616
Where'd you grow up, and
why were you a NASCAR fan?

00:03:10.616 --> 00:03:12.926
- Sure, so, I grew up in Vermont.

00:03:12.926 --> 00:03:16.636
I see on India's book there,
we've got the Vermont sticker.

00:03:16.636 --> 00:03:17.586
Thank you for that.

00:03:19.156 --> 00:03:20.706
There're not a lot of us there.

00:03:21.876 --> 00:03:22.896
- Hold on, I apologize.

00:03:22.896 --> 00:03:26.356
India, why do you have a Vermont sticker?

00:03:26.356 --> 00:03:28.769
- I lived there for four
years in Bennington, Vermont,

00:03:28.769 --> 00:03:29.836
when I was in college.

00:03:29.836 --> 00:03:30.876
- God dammit, I forgot that.

00:03:30.876 --> 00:03:31.859
Okay, keep going.

00:03:33.156 --> 00:03:34.906
- I grew up in Burlington, Vermont.

00:03:37.146 --> 00:03:38.986
So, my dad was a big NASCAR fan.

00:03:38.986 --> 00:03:41.866
There was a track about
15 miles from our house,

00:03:41.866 --> 00:03:44.396
which is no longer there,
called Catamount Speedway.

00:03:44.396 --> 00:03:45.376
- [Gary] Oh, Catamount.

00:03:45.376 --> 00:03:47.523
- And so, I went there the first time

00:03:47.523 --> 00:03:50.076
when I was five years old
with my brother and my dad,

00:03:50.076 --> 00:03:52.476
and my father was nice enough to tell me,

00:03:52.476 --> 00:03:56.266
you absolutely have to root
for the Vermont drivers.

00:03:56.266 --> 00:03:58.186
No one from New York, no one from Quebec,

00:03:58.186 --> 00:03:59.090
you have to root for--

00:03:59.090 --> 00:04:00.366
- No Massholes?

00:04:00.366 --> 00:04:03.946
- No. (laughs)

00:04:03.946 --> 00:04:05.846
- [Gary] Who was your favorite driver?

00:04:06.766 --> 00:04:08.176
- My favorite driver at five

00:04:08.176 --> 00:04:10.266
was the driver of the number five,

00:04:10.266 --> 00:04:13.326
white number five, 'cause
it was speed racer.

00:04:13.326 --> 00:04:14.776
Unfortunately, he was from Quebec,

00:04:14.776 --> 00:04:18.266
so my dad forbid me for
rooting for that driver.

00:04:18.266 --> 00:04:21.136
His name was Cabana.

00:04:21.136 --> 00:04:22.106
- Steve, you know what's super interesting

00:04:22.106 --> 00:04:24.196
about this, me and Mickey just smiled,

00:04:24.196 --> 00:04:27.156
a lot of people, even
just a couple days ago,

00:04:27.156 --> 00:04:29.316
always ask me why do I put up number five

00:04:29.316 --> 00:04:31.476
when I take photos with people?

00:04:31.476 --> 00:04:34.226
Actually, when I chose the Jets
as my favorite team at five,

00:04:34.226 --> 00:04:37.066
my favorite player was
Pat Leahy, the kicker,

00:04:37.066 --> 00:04:39.106
who wore number five, and to this day,

00:04:39.106 --> 00:04:40.846
everything, five is very,

00:04:40.846 --> 00:04:43.686
it's funny that you
were that age when you,

00:04:43.686 --> 00:04:45.606
that's wild, that's funny.

00:04:45.606 --> 00:04:46.496
So, go ahead.

00:04:46.496 --> 00:04:48.036
- I'm the fifth president of NASCAR,

00:04:48.036 --> 00:04:49.536
so maybe there's a five connection.

00:04:49.536 --> 00:04:52.306
- [Gary] Yeah, I like that, numbers game.

00:04:52.306 --> 00:04:53.406
Fun facts.
- But yes,

00:04:54.306 --> 00:04:55.386
NASCAR, like most sports,

00:04:55.386 --> 00:04:58.166
very generational, generational transfer,

00:04:58.166 --> 00:05:00.446
so I started with going to the racetrack,

00:05:00.446 --> 00:05:02.336
and the sights and the
smells and the sounds

00:05:02.336 --> 00:05:04.966
of what it's like to go to a NASCAR track,

00:05:04.966 --> 00:05:05.866
it was a short track,

00:05:05.866 --> 00:05:07.886
as I said, doesn't exist anymore there,

00:05:07.886 --> 00:05:11.126
but that was my first love of the sport,

00:05:11.126 --> 00:05:14.106
and it grew, and so I ebbed
and flowed a little bit.

00:05:14.106 --> 00:05:17.486
When I got into college,
I really got back into it,

00:05:17.486 --> 00:05:21.816
and then got in the sports world.

00:05:21.816 --> 00:05:23.796
I remember a time when Paul Tagliabue,

00:05:23.796 --> 00:05:25.176
who was the commissioner when I was there,

00:05:25.176 --> 00:05:26.873
Paul, he brought me up, and he said,

00:05:26.873 --> 00:05:29.696
"Hey I hear you're our
resident NASCAR expert,"

00:05:29.696 --> 00:05:31.636
and I gave him a tutorial on NASCAR,

00:05:31.636 --> 00:05:32.696
so it was pretty cool.

00:05:32.696 --> 00:05:33.876
- That's rad.

00:05:33.876 --> 00:05:34.709
And how about for you,

00:05:34.709 --> 00:05:38.546
you come into the role,
were you CMO right away,

00:05:38.546 --> 00:05:40.196
10 or 11 years ago, or first in marketing,

00:05:40.196 --> 00:05:41.136
and grew up within it?

00:05:41.136 --> 00:05:42.926
- Yeah, no, we created a group

00:05:42.926 --> 00:05:44.246
when I first started at NASCAR,

00:05:44.246 --> 00:05:47.276
designed to, I mean,
this was, like I said,

00:05:47.276 --> 00:05:48.996
quite a few years ago, but it was designed

00:05:48.996 --> 00:05:50.446
to help the drivers with their brands,

00:05:50.446 --> 00:05:53.526
and so prior to us creating that group,

00:05:53.526 --> 00:05:55.806
and it was probably a
very primitive version

00:05:55.806 --> 00:05:57.426
of what we all do today,

00:05:57.426 --> 00:06:00.156
but it's trying to get
these drivers to understand

00:06:00.156 --> 00:06:02.206
that it's not just about what
the did on the racetrack,

00:06:02.206 --> 00:06:04.606
'cause I think their whole mindset was,

00:06:04.606 --> 00:06:07.546
I get in the car, I go
fast, and I go home,

00:06:07.546 --> 00:06:09.686
I sign a couple autographs,
and I call it a day.

00:06:09.686 --> 00:06:12.366
And obviously today,
what they do in the car

00:06:12.366 --> 00:06:13.826
is almost secondary.

00:06:13.826 --> 00:06:15.276
I mean, you gotta win on the track,

00:06:15.276 --> 00:06:16.806
and you've gotta be a competitor,

00:06:16.806 --> 00:06:19.366
but the things that they do on social,

00:06:19.366 --> 00:06:20.659
engaging with fans.

00:06:21.566 --> 00:06:22.746
- [Gary] Sponsor events.

00:06:22.746 --> 00:06:24.416
- Way more important than it used to be.

00:06:24.416 --> 00:06:25.719
So, we started that.

00:06:26.983 --> 00:06:30.616
When I joined NASCAR,
just a three-person team,

00:06:30.616 --> 00:06:34.036
and now it's grown into our
entire marketing organization

00:06:34.036 --> 00:06:35.556
is built on those driver brands.

00:06:35.556 --> 00:06:39.478
- For everybody who's
listening, knowing the sport,

00:06:39.478 --> 00:06:43.196
what is a good three-minute rant

00:06:43.196 --> 00:06:44.736
on the current state of NASCAR

00:06:44.736 --> 00:06:47.286
for people that are
considering it, or watching it.

00:06:48.926 --> 00:06:50.116
Can you give me a little three minutes

00:06:50.116 --> 00:06:51.686
on the current state of the sport?

00:06:51.686 --> 00:06:52.680
- You want me to rant?

00:06:52.680 --> 00:06:54.256
- Go ahead, rant.

00:06:54.256 --> 00:06:58.536
- So, I would say, we have
been, we had this period of time

00:06:58.536 --> 00:07:03.046
over the three to four years,
where I would say five or six

00:07:03.046 --> 00:07:04.879
of our most popular drivers retired.

00:07:05.986 --> 00:07:08.096
And so, when that happened,

00:07:08.096 --> 00:07:11.230
we knew we had to
refresh the driver base--

00:07:11.230 --> 00:07:12.346
- Yeah, it's funny, for
everybody who's listening,

00:07:12.346 --> 00:07:14.356
knowing what we talk a lot about,

00:07:14.356 --> 00:07:18.636
it happened to the NBA after
the Jordan, Bird, Magic thing.

00:07:18.636 --> 00:07:20.946
My favorite comp for this is wrestling,

00:07:20.946 --> 00:07:23.286
since McMahon controls the narrative,

00:07:23.286 --> 00:07:25.806
but it still comes down to
the actors and the athletes,

00:07:25.806 --> 00:07:28.816
and so after Hogan and
Macho and Andre the Giant,

00:07:28.816 --> 00:07:31.616
there's a gap, and then
The Rock and Stone Cold

00:07:31.616 --> 00:07:33.039
come along, and it reboots.

00:07:35.157 --> 00:07:36.253
- Same thing for us.

00:07:36.253 --> 00:07:37.606
So, we had--
- Dale Jr.

00:07:37.606 --> 00:07:40.886
- Yeah, Junior, Tony Stewart, Jeff Gordon,

00:07:40.886 --> 00:07:42.739
Carl Edwards, Danica Patrick.

00:07:44.136 --> 00:07:46.456
Those are big names in our sport,

00:07:46.456 --> 00:07:48.656
and many had been around for two decades,

00:07:48.656 --> 00:07:52.786
and so for us, like any
other brand, any other sport,

00:07:52.786 --> 00:07:54.516
you refresh the brand.

00:07:54.516 --> 00:07:57.186
So, we got a phenomenal
group of young drivers,

00:07:57.186 --> 00:07:59.446
this crop is both the largest,

00:07:59.446 --> 00:08:02.856
in the skills, the personalities,

00:08:02.856 --> 00:08:04.736
the authenticity of who they are,

00:08:04.736 --> 00:08:06.879
and the great news is, they can win.

00:08:08.586 --> 00:08:12.846
You can have a great
personality, be very charismatic,

00:08:12.846 --> 00:08:15.416
our fans want the drivers to win.

00:08:15.416 --> 00:08:16.555
It helps develop their--

00:08:16.555 --> 00:08:18.196
- This is a debate,

00:08:18.196 --> 00:08:20.499
and pontificating that
I have with you guys.

00:08:21.606 --> 00:08:22.896
So, everybody who's listening,

00:08:22.896 --> 00:08:26.046
my incredible observation
of the next 20, 30 years,

00:08:26.046 --> 00:08:29.916
is that the direct social scale does,

00:08:29.916 --> 00:08:31.606
for the first time ever,

00:08:31.606 --> 00:08:34.196
create a debate of winning on the field

00:08:34.196 --> 00:08:35.416
being a direct attribute,

00:08:35.416 --> 00:08:38.036
because I do think that we're
gonna be able to see 360,

00:08:38.036 --> 00:08:40.376
no different than people who
are vloggers and influencers,

00:08:40.376 --> 00:08:42.366
and they're not winning anything,

00:08:42.366 --> 00:08:45.496
can America or the world become fascinated

00:08:45.496 --> 00:08:49.456
by a human, just based on their
backstory, their life story?

00:08:49.456 --> 00:08:50.896
By the way, you're talking to a sports fan

00:08:50.896 --> 00:08:53.476
who actually drops his
teams when they win.

00:08:53.476 --> 00:08:55.966
I stopped being a Yankee
fan and Rangers fan

00:08:55.966 --> 00:08:58.636
after Rangers won in '94
and the Yankees won in '96,

00:08:58.636 --> 00:09:00.256
so I'm in it for the climb.

00:09:00.256 --> 00:09:02.383
If I got behind a racer, young,

00:09:04.685 --> 00:09:05.826
I'd probably love them two
or three or four years--

00:09:05.826 --> 00:09:08.006
- [Jill] You want the underdog.

00:09:08.006 --> 00:09:10.036
- I want the process of trying to win,

00:09:10.036 --> 00:09:10.886
more so the winner.

00:09:10.886 --> 00:09:12.456
But nonetheless, to your point,

00:09:12.456 --> 00:09:16.386
winning has always been the
variable for max popularity,

00:09:16.386 --> 00:09:18.246
but you guys have a pedigree sport.

00:09:18.246 --> 00:09:20.196
One thing that interesting about you guys,

00:09:20.196 --> 00:09:22.136
the Elliott kid now, Dale Jr.,

00:09:22.136 --> 00:09:23.916
I remember watching that very carefully,

00:09:23.916 --> 00:09:26.259
as a casual fan just knowing.

00:09:27.386 --> 00:09:30.826
When you are a second
generation of an icon,

00:09:30.826 --> 00:09:32.796
you definitely have a pretty big fan base

00:09:32.796 --> 00:09:34.096
before you've won,

00:09:34.096 --> 00:09:38.736
which I always feel super compassionate

00:09:38.736 --> 00:09:40.106
and empathetic towards that kid,

00:09:40.106 --> 00:09:42.306
'cause the pressure's impossible.

00:09:42.306 --> 00:09:44.436
- Well, you look at Dale
Jr. early in his career,

00:09:44.436 --> 00:09:48.146
and I think part of why he has transformed

00:09:48.146 --> 00:09:50.126
into such a great media personality

00:09:50.126 --> 00:09:53.716
is that early on, you have
to think that pressure

00:09:53.716 --> 00:09:55.716
was a lot for him to handle,

00:09:55.716 --> 00:09:57.686
and I think as he continued to get older

00:09:57.686 --> 00:10:00.076
and then even now, when he's in the booth,

00:10:00.076 --> 00:10:02.546
broadcasting races, he's
much more comfortable

00:10:02.546 --> 00:10:05.056
with his role and his legacy,

00:10:05.056 --> 00:10:07.386
and you can see that in how he interacts.

00:10:07.386 --> 00:10:08.876
- Yeah, it's a tough piece.

00:10:08.876 --> 00:10:11.169
I mean, Petty's kid raced, right?

00:10:12.126 --> 00:10:14.696
And like not as successfully of a career.

00:10:14.696 --> 00:10:15.529
It's tough.

00:10:17.646 --> 00:10:18.869
- Richard Petty, King,

00:10:20.156 --> 00:10:22.236
no one's gonna, that's a level--

00:10:22.236 --> 00:10:23.433
- Have you met the King?

00:10:23.433 --> 00:10:24.799
- I have not, I'd love to.

00:10:24.799 --> 00:10:26.618
- [Jill] We need to make that happen.

00:10:26.618 --> 00:10:28.146
- [Gary] What is he, 43?

00:10:28.146 --> 00:10:28.979
- 43.

00:10:28.979 --> 00:10:30.746
- See, to me, that's interesting.

00:10:30.746 --> 00:10:32.256
The fact that I know that

00:10:32.256 --> 00:10:36.046
just speaks to how much of
an impact he had on culture.

00:10:36.046 --> 00:10:39.336
Yeah, I mean listen, any time
your dad is known in anything

00:10:39.336 --> 00:10:41.943
as the fucking King, you're in deep shit.

00:10:41.943 --> 00:10:43.146
Let's just get to the punchline.

00:10:43.146 --> 00:10:44.376
Like, that's gonna be a problem.

00:10:44.376 --> 00:10:47.380
Bad news Xander, marketing
is gonna be tough for you.

00:10:47.380 --> 00:10:48.286
I'm kidding, Dustin.

00:10:48.286 --> 00:10:49.786
All right, let's keep going.

00:10:49.786 --> 00:10:50.996
- I think the interesting thing, too,

00:10:50.996 --> 00:10:54.016
so you talk about these
young stars that we have,

00:10:54.016 --> 00:10:56.566
and authentic and charismatic,

00:10:56.566 --> 00:10:58.986
you take the guy who currently runs

00:10:58.986 --> 00:11:01.726
for Richard Petty
Motorsports, Bubba Wallace,

00:11:01.726 --> 00:11:04.636
African-American kid,
phenomenal personality,

00:11:04.636 --> 00:11:07.956
and not afraid to show his emotions,

00:11:07.956 --> 00:11:12.306
not afraid, I mean he's
funny, he's just a cool guy.

00:11:12.306 --> 00:11:13.758
- [Gary] Wanna get some nerdy ones in.

00:11:13.758 --> 00:11:15.786
Keep going, I'm listening.
- You've got

00:11:15.786 --> 00:11:17.596
Chase Elliott, you have Danny Suarez,

00:11:17.596 --> 00:11:19.836
who's from Mexico, he's in the series.

00:11:19.836 --> 00:11:24.526
So, we've got these young up-and-coming

00:11:24.526 --> 00:11:27.706
winners on the racetrack
that can wheel a race car

00:11:27.706 --> 00:11:29.436
that's resonating with our fan base.

00:11:29.436 --> 00:11:32.176
So, right now, I believe this is,

00:11:32.176 --> 00:11:34.376
maybe I'm not an expert to say this,

00:11:34.376 --> 00:11:36.336
but just my own eye
test, I think the racing

00:11:36.336 --> 00:11:37.966
that we have right now on our racetracks

00:11:37.966 --> 00:11:39.226
is the best we've ever had.

00:11:39.226 --> 00:11:42.276
- You're also going through
a lot of innovation.

00:11:42.276 --> 00:11:45.286
One of things that I admire from afar

00:11:45.286 --> 00:11:48.236
that the sport's doing is, look,

00:11:48.236 --> 00:11:51.266
by losing those big names

00:11:51.266 --> 00:11:54.876
by different TV dynamics
over the last 15 years,

00:11:54.876 --> 00:11:58.526
10 years, we're not at the apex of NASCAR

00:11:58.526 --> 00:12:01.489
as we sit here today in
the way that we were.

00:12:03.193 --> 00:12:04.896
You've been there 14 years, you're a fan,

00:12:04.896 --> 00:12:09.896
what is the last real
white-hot era, would you say?

00:12:10.176 --> 00:12:14.206
- I would say probably '07 to '09.

00:12:14.206 --> 00:12:15.039
- Yeah.

00:12:16.726 --> 00:12:18.876
Was that when Nextel was--

00:12:18.876 --> 00:12:22.576
- [Jill] Nextel came in
in 2004 with that season.

00:12:22.576 --> 00:12:26.719
- So, what's really interesting is,

00:12:26.719 --> 00:12:29.496
this is something I yell
about with baseball,

00:12:29.496 --> 00:12:32.868
and I think they're finally
starting, and I have empathy,

00:12:32.868 --> 00:12:34.826
when you start getting
into 200 years and shit,

00:12:34.826 --> 00:12:37.679
but you guys are really innovating.

00:12:38.542 --> 00:12:40.686
You guys are doing things that,

00:12:40.686 --> 00:12:43.196
I kinda slightly am curious

00:12:43.196 --> 00:12:46.506
what the old school 30, 40,
50-year fan is thinking,

00:12:46.506 --> 00:12:48.766
when you start changing sports,

00:12:48.766 --> 00:12:50.806
heck, I'm a diehard football fan,

00:12:50.806 --> 00:12:53.506
and I weirdly don't
want an 18-game schedule

00:12:53.506 --> 00:12:55.386
for one weird reason:

00:12:55.386 --> 00:12:58.969
It throws off me understanding
how to calibrate stats.

00:12:59.989 --> 00:13:02.186
So, change is hard,

00:13:02.186 --> 00:13:04.546
but you guys have absolutely
shown the willingness

00:13:04.546 --> 00:13:06.546
and have announced more changes to come,

00:13:07.642 --> 00:13:08.526
and from a business standpoint,

00:13:08.526 --> 00:13:10.386
I'm always innovate or die,

00:13:10.386 --> 00:13:13.006
that has not been the historic
point of view of sports

00:13:13.006 --> 00:13:15.789
at all, and I feel like you guys,

00:13:16.766 --> 00:13:19.486
I'll let you talk, but
outside of rogue leagues,

00:13:19.486 --> 00:13:21.186
not rogue leagues, other leagues,

00:13:21.186 --> 00:13:24.776
most of basketball's
innovation came from the ABA.

00:13:24.776 --> 00:13:26.956
Most of the innovation for the NFL

00:13:26.956 --> 00:13:29.466
came from the USFL or the XFL.

00:13:29.466 --> 00:13:32.756
It usually comes when
you see a pop of that.

00:13:32.756 --> 00:13:34.386
You guys are doing it to yourself.

00:13:34.386 --> 00:13:35.219
- We are.

00:13:36.226 --> 00:13:37.446
For us, we think it was important

00:13:37.446 --> 00:13:39.966
to do exactly that, because...

00:13:43.890 --> 00:13:45.376
existing fans and then new fans,

00:13:45.376 --> 00:13:48.766
fans you're trying to
get to watch our sport,

00:13:48.766 --> 00:13:51.096
we need to make sure
that they're entertained,

00:13:51.096 --> 00:13:53.956
and we've been criticized about

00:13:53.956 --> 00:13:55.466
hey, you're chasing the entertainment,

00:13:55.466 --> 00:13:58.196
and you're chasing, the answer is yes,

00:13:58.196 --> 00:13:59.836
it needs to be compelling.

00:13:59.836 --> 00:14:02.566
And so we have created
things like stage racing,

00:14:02.566 --> 00:14:05.936
so three different segments of our races,

00:14:05.936 --> 00:14:08.306
and then there are points
you can get as a driver

00:14:08.306 --> 00:14:10.136
that help you get to our playoffs,

00:14:10.136 --> 00:14:13.586
so playoffs, new concept
for us that we introduced.

00:14:13.586 --> 00:14:15.759
Segments, new concept that we introduced.

00:14:16.881 --> 00:14:19.786
The race car itself, and
what we're doing today,

00:14:19.786 --> 00:14:21.926
and where we're going with
the race car in the future.

00:14:21.926 --> 00:14:23.676
- What's been the reaction to that?

00:14:24.869 --> 00:14:26.576
I mean, it's always pro
and con, I'm curious.

00:14:26.576 --> 00:14:30.189
- I promise I'll let you
talk, but I get excited.

00:14:31.430 --> 00:14:32.686
- You grew up a fan, Jill didn't.

00:14:32.686 --> 00:14:33.595
This is appropriate.

00:14:33.595 --> 00:14:35.686
- No, I had three cousins that dragged me

00:14:35.686 --> 00:14:37.466
to the local dirt tracks.

00:14:37.466 --> 00:14:39.246
They're all just mad at me still today

00:14:39.246 --> 00:14:40.426
that I have this job and they don't,

00:14:40.426 --> 00:14:43.326
so shout out to the crew.

00:14:43.326 --> 00:14:45.086
- I think, for us,

00:14:45.086 --> 00:14:47.829
a lot of the things
that we have introduced,

00:14:49.626 --> 00:14:51.456
typically it's a 50-50, right?

00:14:51.456 --> 00:14:54.206
So, you have half your fans
that say hey I like that,

00:14:54.206 --> 00:14:55.776
and half don't.

00:14:55.776 --> 00:14:57.386
And so what we've been able to do,

00:14:57.386 --> 00:15:00.016
because we do a lot of research

00:15:00.016 --> 00:15:01.836
and a lot of analytics and insights

00:15:01.836 --> 00:15:05.226
in terms of what we
believe is going to work,

00:15:05.226 --> 00:15:08.426
and sometimes, fans don't
know what is going to work.

00:15:08.426 --> 00:15:10.766
They're kind of stuck in
hey, this is what I like,

00:15:10.766 --> 00:15:12.596
and so change is hard.

00:15:12.596 --> 00:15:14.156
- If I listen to my customer,

00:15:14.156 --> 00:15:16.416
I would've made a faster horse.

00:15:16.416 --> 00:15:18.186
And you guys are, I'm always
like listen to the customer,

00:15:18.186 --> 00:15:19.536
but then I always counter it with that,

00:15:19.536 --> 00:15:20.369
that's just the truth.

00:15:20.369 --> 00:15:23.286
- So for us, it's kind
of what happens, right?

00:15:23.286 --> 00:15:26.446
So, we've been able to, in
a lot of different things

00:15:26.446 --> 00:15:28.436
that we've done, so playoffs,

00:15:28.436 --> 00:15:30.736
more than eight out of 10 of our fans

00:15:30.736 --> 00:15:33.226
say hey, that's a great
idea, started at 50.

00:15:33.226 --> 00:15:35.616
Stage racing, started at 50,

00:15:35.616 --> 00:15:37.336
now we're well up over 80%,

00:15:37.336 --> 00:15:40.431
so we're always trying
to listen to the fan

00:15:40.431 --> 00:15:41.969
and kinda iterate around it.

00:15:43.530 --> 00:15:44.926
- Before we get to this call,

00:15:44.926 --> 00:15:46.856
what's been the most interesting thing

00:15:46.856 --> 00:15:49.456
in the last year or two
from a marketing standpoint?

00:15:50.479 --> 00:15:51.646
It can be completely anything,

00:15:51.646 --> 00:15:54.246
it can be a tactic you did on Instagram,

00:15:54.246 --> 00:15:55.849
it can be a brand coming to you,

00:15:56.766 --> 00:15:59.196
just hot take, some of
the interesting stuff

00:15:59.196 --> 00:16:01.216
on the intersection of
marketing and NASCAR.

00:16:01.216 --> 00:16:02.986
- A lot of what we work with you guys on,

00:16:02.986 --> 00:16:05.336
I mean the explosion of content,

00:16:05.336 --> 00:16:07.176
we would never say that it was good

00:16:07.176 --> 00:16:10.006
that we had the retirement
of all those big stars.

00:16:10.006 --> 00:16:12.586
That clearly was not the case.

00:16:12.586 --> 00:16:14.216
- Adversity leads to innovation.

00:16:14.216 --> 00:16:17.516
- Right, and given where
the world is going,

00:16:17.516 --> 00:16:20.566
these young drivers are
eager to prove themselves,

00:16:20.566 --> 00:16:22.766
they're out there, they're social natives,

00:16:22.766 --> 00:16:24.686
they are ready to take on the challenge,

00:16:24.686 --> 00:16:26.937
so we would've had a harder
time I think adapting--

00:16:26.937 --> 00:16:28.336
- With the OGs.

00:16:28.336 --> 00:16:29.996
- Yeah, with those veteran drivers

00:16:29.996 --> 00:16:32.556
that are like you know what,
I know what I need to do,

00:16:32.556 --> 00:16:33.666
so I'm good.

00:16:33.666 --> 00:16:35.466
- This has been working
for me for 20 years,

00:16:35.466 --> 00:16:37.216
screw you, Jill.

00:16:37.216 --> 00:16:38.986
You Tweet, get out of my face.

00:16:38.986 --> 00:16:41.776
- I'm busy, I have a lot
of engagements, yeah.

00:16:41.776 --> 00:16:43.177
- Who's this?

00:16:43.177 --> 00:16:44.223
- Beanz and Rice.

00:16:44.223 --> 00:16:45.119
